WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.388 --> 00:00:06.350
This building contains a truly gigantic laser.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.506
The National Ignition Facility...

00:00:08.506 --> 00:00:10.095
is about a hundred feet tall...

00:00:10.095 --> 00:00:12.323
as big as three football fields...

00:00:12.323 --> 00:00:17.085
And packed to the brim with tubes and wires
that look the bowels of a spaceship.

00:00:17.085 --> 00:00:20.440
It makes sense that one of the Star Trek movies
shot a few scenes there.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.990
"Do you know what this is?!"

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.693
"It’s a warp core."

00:00:23.693 --> 00:00:26.230
Actually, it’s NIF’s “target chamber”.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.820
The laser beam starts over here in this room
as a tiny pulse of light

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.009
about as weak as a regular laser pointer.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.590
But then it bounces around the building

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.527
getting amplified over and over until
it’s a quadrillion times stronger.

00:00:37.527 --> 00:00:40.170
And then, it’s focused onto a single point.

00:00:41.795 --> 00:00:44.607
So... what do you do with a laser this big?

00:00:44.607 --> 00:00:49.600
Well, right now, scientists are using it
to study the secret inner lives of planets.

00:00:54.673 --> 00:00:57.030
It started in second grade, when I was younger

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.130
my teacher showed me pictures of Jupiter.

00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:03.339
I remember in class vividly that she said

00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:05.760
that no one knows what's on the inside
of the planet.

00:01:06.446 --> 00:01:09.740
I was like oh well, maybe I could be
the one that figures that out!

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.990
Now, Tanja Kovecivic is a planetary scientist
at UC Berkeley

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.665
and she and her colleagues are asking 
broader questions.

00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:17.950
Why are we here?

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.359
How did our solar system form?

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:21.970
How planets form, how the moon formed

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:24.584
and especially now with the search
for exoplanets...

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:27.065
How do we find a habitable planet?

00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:28.801
What does that look like?

00:01:28.801 --> 00:01:33.550
How did earth or anything else go from
a molten ball

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.530
to something that we can all now survive on.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.000
The features that define Earth:
its rich atmosphere...

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.110
its vast oceans...

00:01:41.832 --> 00:01:43.315
its magnetic field...

00:01:43.459 --> 00:01:45.840
its solid but shifting crust...

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.540
these features that fostered 
life as we know it

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.640
they were shaped by unseen processes
hidden deep within the globe.

00:01:53.543 --> 00:01:56.900
If we want to understand the engines
that sculpted Earth, or Jupiter

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.219
or the thousands of recently discovered worlds

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:02.442
we need to look under the hood.

00:02:02.442 --> 00:02:04.157
But that’s not easy.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:08.056
On the surface of the Earth, we exist under
very unique conditions.

00:02:08.056 --> 00:02:13.494
We’re here, in this tiny range of relatively
low temperatures and low pressures.

00:02:13.494 --> 00:02:14.891
But if we drill deeper.

00:02:14.891 --> 00:02:19.674
Most of the matter, even within Earth,
exists under extreme temperatures and pressures.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.511
A trip into Jupiter is even more intense.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.520
At these depths, the laws of physics 
predict that

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.870
familiar substances will start to act...

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:30.870
pretty weird.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.170
When you're subjecting matter to intense pressure,
you're squishing it right?

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:39.426
So you're actually causing the atoms
to re-orient themselves.

00:02:39.426 --> 00:02:41.700
When you heat it up, you're
also causing vibration.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.930
You're just kind of irritating this thing
into being a whole new material sometimes.

00:02:46.742 --> 00:02:50.071
For a long time, physicists have theorized
that deep within a planet

00:02:50.071 --> 00:02:53.002
iron can flow like water.

00:02:53.002 --> 00:02:57.313
And those swirling currents generate
Earth’s magnetic field.

00:02:57.313 --> 00:03:01.548
Also in theory, sodium can turn
completely transparent.

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:05.854
Water can form a hot black ice
that conducts electricity.

00:03:05.854 --> 00:03:09.470
And hydrogen condenses into a metallic liquid.

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:15.910
To understand the strange chemistry and geology
and physics happening inside planets

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.806
we have to study substances we’d 
never encounter on the surface.

00:03:19.806 --> 00:03:22.377
And it’s not like we can just go down there
in an elevator.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.459
The closest humans have gotten 
to the Earth’s core

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:28.870
is the Kola Superdeep Borehole
in Northwest Russia:

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.459
7.6 miles straight down.

00:03:31.856 --> 00:03:35.483
But that’s less than 0.2% of the way
to the center of the Earth.

00:03:35.483 --> 00:03:38.560
A probe we shot into Jupiter, stopped transmitting
when it was just

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.999
0.1% of the way to that planet’s core.

00:03:40.999 --> 00:03:46.778
Our instruments can’t survive in the very
temperatures and pressures we want to study.

00:03:46.778 --> 00:03:50.599
But scientists have come up with a few
different ways to get around this problem.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.992
The first is to create detailed computer models.

00:03:55.418 --> 00:03:59.123
So we have oxygen or little white hydrogens.

00:03:59.123 --> 00:04:02.020
All the little brown ones are going to magnesium.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:03.603
You can look at it in 3D.

00:04:03.603 --> 00:04:05.157
Oh it rotates!

00:04:05.157 --> 00:04:06.157
Yeah.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.630
Tanja programmed this collection of 
300 some atoms.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:14.204
It’s a tiny microcosm of a barrier between
rock and ice inside a planet.

00:04:14.204 --> 00:04:18.310
Her model simulates how each atom
and every last one of its electrons

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.089
will act and interact.

00:04:20.486 --> 00:04:23.805
It’s a simulation of just a few billionths
of a second.

00:04:23.805 --> 00:04:27.150
But it takes one of the most powerful supercomputers
in the world

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.168
a bit longer than that to complete.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:30.332
138 hours.

00:04:30.332 --> 00:04:31.447
OK gotcha.

00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:35.529
Little by little, painstaking simulations
like these are starting to paint a picture

00:04:35.529 --> 00:04:38.024
of what’s happening inside planets.

00:04:38.024 --> 00:04:40.889
And they’re helping guide experiments
in the real world.

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:44.590
We don't have that much time and facilities
that we can sort of go out and

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.253
sample all kinds of worlds material that
all the conditions.

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:50.580
And so they say hey, maybe you should look
in this direction.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.403
There could be something interesting there.

00:04:52.403 --> 00:04:54.240
So we have all these questions.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.092
We have all these theories.

00:04:57.013 --> 00:05:01.162
But we want to confirm that with
“real world experiments”.

00:05:01.162 --> 00:05:02.054
Yeah.

00:05:02.054 --> 00:05:03.686
What does that look like?

00:05:03.686 --> 00:05:05.330
That looks like a few different things:

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.661
the first one of those would be
a diamond anvil cell.

00:05:07.661 --> 00:05:09.900
You take very high purity diamonds.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.601
You think of the diamond in the 
diamond wedding band.

00:05:12.601 --> 00:05:15.833
You basically compress a sample
within just the between the two.

00:05:16.418 --> 00:05:20.090
This setup can create some of the temperatures
and pressures seen inside earth.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.629
And if you want to go further, you can use...

00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:24.083
The gas gun method.

00:05:24.083 --> 00:05:25.369
With a gas gun

00:05:25.369 --> 00:05:29.351
you're launching basically a plate of metal
into the sample that you care about.

00:05:32.132 --> 00:05:34.373
Temperature and pressure spike on impact.

00:05:35.221 --> 00:05:36.259
That’s pretty good.

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:40.460
But sometimes, you want to peer even deeper
into a giant planet.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:45.286
The third, and kind of most extreme of those
would be laser compression.

00:05:45.286 --> 00:05:48.880
And for that, you need a truly gigantic laser.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:52.469
NIF wasn’t built to explore other worlds
— not at all -

00:05:52.469 --> 00:05:54.625
it was built to save our own.

00:05:54.625 --> 00:05:58.219
In the 1970s, scientists were trying to harness
nuclear fusion:

00:05:58.219 --> 00:06:00.439
the reaction that powers our sun.

00:06:00.439 --> 00:06:03.319
It would be cheap, it would be clean,
it would be...

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:05.419
the ultimate energy.

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:09.990
The dream was (and still is) to hit
a tiny fuel pellet with powerful lasers

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.474
and create a miniature star here on earth

00:06:12.474 --> 00:06:18.409
"We need to build a laser 1000 times more
powerful than any laser presently available."

00:06:18.409 --> 00:06:23.119
Scientists have spent decades designing
and building larger and larger lasers.

00:06:23.119 --> 00:06:25.430
Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory built NIF. 

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.890
And the University of Rochester built their giant
OMEGA laser system. 

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.869
These facilities are still plugging away at 
understanding nuclear fusion  

00:06:33.869 --> 00:06:38.696
but they've also become important tools for
astrophysicists and material scientists.

00:06:38.696 --> 00:06:43.006
Researchers at the Center for Matters at Atomic Pressures
use OMEGA and NIF

00:06:43.006 --> 00:06:45.999
to recreate the interiors of planets and stars

00:06:45.999 --> 00:06:48.902
and create revolutionary states of matter.

00:06:49.608 --> 00:06:52.813
Instead of loading up a target chamber
with pellets of fusion fuel

00:06:52.813 --> 00:06:55.689
they put in a sample, like a tiny speck 
of iron.

00:06:55.689 --> 00:06:58.069
We have to place it inside of this
little apparatus

00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:00.650
which you can actually hold in
the palm of your hand.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.108
It looks like this.

00:07:02.108 --> 00:07:06.743
And it happens to be the same semicircular
shape as UC Berkeley’s Greek Theater.

00:07:06.743 --> 00:07:09.283
Which is where I interviewed Mercedes.

00:07:09.283 --> 00:07:12.300
So if we imagine that we're standing inside
of the apparatus

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.469
we could imagine this would be the sample

00:07:14.469 --> 00:07:15.929
not much thicker than a hair.

00:07:15.929 --> 00:07:17.398
—Teeny-tiny.
—Yeah.

00:07:17.398 --> 00:07:21.710
So our sample is mounted right on the front
of that little hole in the center there.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:25.245
Which, in the Greek Theater, would be at
the doors at the back of the stage.

00:07:25.245 --> 00:07:29.234
Then we would actually have another layer
of material right behind it.

00:07:29.234 --> 00:07:31.020
We call that layer the ablator.

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:35.146
Our laser is going to come in from this direction...

00:07:39.569 --> 00:07:42.108
they're going to hit the back surface 
of this ablator.

00:07:42.108 --> 00:07:45.394
That's going to cause a kind of a 
mini localized explosion...

00:07:45.394 --> 00:07:47.819
Which drives the compression waves 
towards our sample.

00:07:47.819 --> 00:07:51.461
The temperature and pressure 
within the sample skyrockets.

00:07:51.461 --> 00:07:55.330
You'd have to have a comet colliding
at 20-30,000 miles an hour

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:59.409
to start reaching the conditions that NIF
can do on a daily basis.

00:07:59.409 --> 00:08:02.849
The experiment only lasts about 30 billionths
of a second

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:06.448
but that’s long enough to
take an X-ray snapshot.

00:08:06.448 --> 00:08:08.826
The x-rays bounce off the sample...

00:08:10.144 --> 00:08:14.610
and leave patterns on the walls of the device
that scientists can later decode.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:18.130
The x-rays are telling us what's going on
inside the sample, you know, is it melting?

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.415
Does it stay solid?

00:08:19.415 --> 00:08:22.683
Is it changing the arrangement 
of the atoms itself?

00:08:22.683 --> 00:08:27.959
Using NIF, Rick saw that iron will be a liquid
under pressures found in giant rocky planets

00:08:27.959 --> 00:08:32.949
much bigger than Earth – and so planets
like that could also have magnetic fields.

00:08:32.949 --> 00:08:37.220
Other high pressure experiments have shown
that sodium does become clear.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.780
And water can form hot, black ice.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.418
And hydrogen does turn metallic.

00:08:42.418 --> 00:08:45.722
Scientists think there’s an immense ocean
of this form of hydrogen

00:08:45.722 --> 00:08:47.760
beneath Jupiter’s clouds

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.756
and its currents generate Jupiter’s 
magnetic field.

00:08:51.756 --> 00:08:57.410
All these phases of matter that we had predicted,
potentially existed

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.870
now we're able to theoretically prove through
models and through experimentation

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:02.330
that they do exist.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.480
By peering at a handful of atoms

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.460
a little crumb of matter - for just a tiny
fraction of a second

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:12.650
scientists are unraveling the billion year
histories of massive planets

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.853
millions of miles away.

